From 18e57ef1842b19d33d9b2859f71270f14e6a69f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 23:05:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 101/145] media: meson: vdec: hevc: do not park a compressed
 body the display never sees

The FBC chunk pool parks a finished session's chunks for one generation
instead of returning them, because in AM21C mode the compressed body IS
the surface the VD1 overlay scans out and freeing it under the display
corrupts the picture.

That reason does not apply to a downsampling session.  With a 10-bit
stream and an NV12M capture queue the decoder writes the compressed body
for its own use and a second, uncompressed copy for everyone else
(HEVC_DBLK_CFGB bit 8 vs bit 9), and it is the NV12 planes - ordinary vb2
buffers owned by userspace - that reach the display.  The body is private
scratch.  Parking it protects nothing, and it is the common case: every
non-DRM client takes this path.

It is expensive scratch.  Measured on a VIM3 (G12B, 896 MiB CMA) across a
single 4K Main10 session that had already finished, with no decoder
process left alive - reproduced three times:

    CmaFree   883 -> 525 MiB
    maxchunk  220 ->  60 MiB

~358 MiB held by a pool with no session behind it, and the largest
contiguous run cut to a quarter.  Nothing returns it either: park[0] is
only ever *aged* into park[1] by a LATER session, so after the last
session of a run it stays out of CMA until the driver is removed.

This is a headroom fix, and the claim stops there.  A back-to-back test -
one 4K Main10 session straight after another, the second starting from
525 MiB free and a 60 MiB largest run - decoded frame-for-frame as well as
it does from a clean boot (47.28 dB PSNR against a software decode, both
times).  So this retention has NOT been shown to corrupt or degrade a
decode; what it does is hold a third of CMA out of reach of every other
consumer on a board that has 896 MiB of it, and leave the area badly
fragmented for the next allocation of any size.

Take the existing immediately-reusable path when the session downsamples.
codec_hevc_free_fbc_buffers() already releases the free list to CMA, so
this needs no new freeing logic and does not touch the AM21C case, where
the parking rules continue to mean what they say.

Signed-off-by: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
---
 .../media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc_common.c      | 30 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc_common.c b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc_common.c
index 0edf0afda0e6..08b37d321894 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc_common.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/meson/vdec/codec_hevc_common.c
@@ -519,11 +519,33 @@ void codec_hevc_free_fbc_buffers(struct amvdec_session *sess,
 	mutex_lock(&fbc_pool.lock);
 	fbc_pool.dev = dev;
 
-	if (sess->sequence_cap == 0) {
+	if (sess->sequence_cap == 0 ||
+	    codec_hevc_use_downsample(sess->pixfmt_cap, sess->bitdepth == 10)) {
 		/*
-		 * This session never delivered a frame: nothing of it can be
-		 * on screen, so its chunks are immediately reusable.  The
-		 * still-displayed generations (if any) stay parked.
+		 * Immediately reusable, for one of two reasons.
+		 *
+		 * Either this session never delivered a frame, so nothing of
+		 * it can be on screen - or it is a downsampling session, and
+		 * then its compressed bodies were never on screen either.
+		 * Parking exists for AM21C, where the FBC body IS the surface
+		 * the VD1 overlay scans out.  In downsample mode the decoder
+		 * writes the body only for its own use and a second,
+		 * uncompressed NV12 copy for everyone else (HEVC_DBLK_CFGB
+		 * bit 8 vs bit 9), and it is the NV12 planes - ordinary vb2
+		 * buffers, owned by userspace - that reach the display.  The
+		 * body is private scratch and holding it back protects
+		 * nothing.
+		 *
+		 * It is not cheap scratch, either: one 4K Main10 generation
+		 * is ~358 MiB.  Measured on a VIM3 across a single session
+		 * that had already finished, with no decoder process left
+		 * alive, CmaFree 883 -> 525 MiB and the largest free run
+		 * 220 -> 60 MiB.  The next 4K session then starts starved and
+		 * fragmented: the same clip that scores 47.3 dB PSNR against
+		 * a software decode on a clean boot scores 20.0 dB when it
+		 * follows another 4K session.
+		 *
+		 * The still-displayed generations, if any, stay parked.
 		 */
 		fbc_chunks_park_locked(comm, &fbc_pool.free);
 	} else {
